Transcribe this page. It is page {{ page }} of a document whose text could not
be extracted, so what you write is the only version of this page anything else
will ever read.

Return the page as markdown, and nothing else. No preamble, no summary, no
note about what you were asked to do.

Rules, in the order they matter:

1. **Transcribe, do not describe.** Reproduce the words that are on the page,
   in the language and script they are written in. Do not translate, correct
   spelling, expand abbreviations, or tidy the phrasing. A misspelling in a
   source document is evidence.
2. **A figure gets a line, not a paragraph.** Where the page holds a chart,
   photograph, diagram, map, stamp or signature, write
   `[Figure: what it shows]` in the place it appears. Include any text inside
   it — axis labels, captions, legends, dates — because that text is
   searchable and the description is not.
3. **Keep the structure the page has.** Headings as headings, lists as lists,
   tables as markdown tables. Where a table will not survive as one, keep the
   rows readable line by line rather than flattening them into prose.
4. **Mark what you cannot read** as `[illegible]`, in place. Do not guess at
   a word, a digit, or a date. A wrong number transcribed confidently is worse
   than a gap, because nothing downstream can tell it was a guess.
5. **Nothing that is not on the page.** No context, no inference about what
   the document is, no filling in a sentence the page cuts off.

If the page carries no text and no figure — a blank page, a scanning artefact
— return `[Page {{ page }}: blank]` and nothing else.
